The symbol tables mrbc -S writes are constants

mrbc -S writes compiled Ruby as static C structs, which every interpreter in
a process runs. A symbol with no MRB_SYM-style name ($!, :"a b") was left 0
in its table and interned by each interpreter as it started, into that one
table. Interpreters starting on different threads wrote it at the same time,
and one starting wrote it while another read it: a DAW holds several
instances of a plugin, each with its own interpreter on a thread of its own.
ThreadSanitizer reported it on every run of sp_instances_check.c (six
writes, all of $!, 26 November 2026). The value written was the same each
time, since $! is numbered at build time, so nothing went wrong; it was a
data race all the same, and the tables could not be const.

Every symbol is named by a macro now. MRB_XSYM names one by its bytes in
hex, MRB_XSYM(2421) for $!, and the build numbers it as it does the rest;
a global variable with a word for a name ($stdout) takes MRB_GVSYM, which
the dump did not use. Nothing is written when an interpreter starts and
every table is const. The build writes its symbol headers again when
lib/mruby/presym.rb is newer than they are: the names it gives can change
while the list of symbols does not, as here, and a build over an existing
one kept the old header. With a test in mrbc's bintest.

Left as it was: with debug information, a dump still fills in each file's
name as an interpreter starts (cdump_debug). The runtime's mruby is built
without it.

Written as it would be for upstream; applied to a copy of the submodule by
scripts/build-runtime.sh, never to the submodule.

